exports[`transcript Markdown, every corpus page > afterReport 1`] = `
"# What happens after you report a housing or pest problem — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Information → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`afterReport\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/information/what-happens-after-you-report-housing-pest-problem\` · **Type:** Information

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Page title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Page title:**

What happens after you report a housing or pest problem

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Learn how a 311 report is reviewed and when an inspector may contact you.

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — supply the parent Agency page by hand. Where the mockup names the agency it is text, not a page reference, and Karl requires a page: for HHVC pages that is sf.gov/departments--healthy-housing-and-vector-control._

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Who this information is for

**Text:**

- Anyone who filed a 311 report or is waiting to hear back about a pest or housing problem.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Who can report

**Text:**

Anyone can report a housing or pest problem to 311.

- You can report anonymously. 311 does not require your name.
- You can report even if you are not the tenant. A friend, family member, advocate, or helper can report for someone else.
- You can ask 311 for help in your language.
- If you rent, tell your landlord first, unless the problem is urgent. [Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions](tenantRights) explains how long to wait.

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions"

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

How a report moves through the City

**Text:**

1. **You report through 311.** 311 receives the report and sends it to the right City department when possible.
2. **Environmental Health reviews the report.** HHVC may review the complaint if it may involve a housing or pest-related public health concern. 311 routes the complaint to Environmental Health, and HHVC assigns it to an inspector. This can take a few days. Complaints are processed on weekdays.
3. **Another agency may take it.** If your complaint involves structural water intrusion, HHVC routes it to the Department of Building Inspection (DBI). That includes leaking roof frames, structural plumbing leaks, and broken water heaters. DBI handles these under the San Francisco Housing Code (2025). HHVC focuses strictly on condensation, humidity, and ventilation-related moisture issues.
4. **An inspector may contact you.** If you gave contact information, an inspector may contact you to ask questions or schedule a visit.
5. **An inspection may happen.** If you did not give contact information, an inspection may still happen without notice when areas can be accessed. This is more likely if the report describes an urgent health or safety risk. To get ready for a visit, follow [Report a pest problem, start to finish](stepPestReport).

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Report a pest problem, start to finish" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Report a pest problem, start to finish"

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

How to follow up on your report

**Text:**

You can check on a report you already made. It helps to keep a few details handy.

- Keep your 311 service request number if you have one.
- Note the date you made the report.
- Contact 311 to ask about the status of your request.
- Tell 311 if the problem gets worse or becomes urgent.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Tenant rights and retaliation

**Text:**

You can report a condition in a building you rent. We never share your name or contact information.

If you are worried your landlord will retaliate, the tenant rights page explains what retaliation can look like and where to get help.

- [Tenant rights and reporting](tenantRights)

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Tenant rights and reporting" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions"

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

If a problem is found

**Text:**

If an inspection identifies a violation, Environmental Health will issue a Notice of Violation (NOV) to the responsible parties. The NOV will outline required corrections and set a specific compliance deadline based on severity.

**Complaint → Inspection → Notice of Violation → Compliance period → Follow-up inspection → Reinspection fee → Citation or Director's Hearing if unresolved.**

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Deadlines to fix a violation

**Text:**

These timelines represent general enforcement standards. Actual correction deadlines are established on the official Notice of Violation based on the severity of the health hazard.

- **Sewage Backups (48 to 72 hours):** Raw sewage backups need immediate correction. The standard 30-day compliance window does not apply.
- **All Other Violations (30 days):** Most cited conditions must be corrected within 30 days. These include overgrown vegetation, trash piles, rodent harborages, general sanitation problems, minor gaps or cracks, and mold remediation.
- **Bed Bug Treatment:** A property owner or manager must initiate professional treatment within 2 working days of confirmation.
- **Extensions:** HHVC may offer an extension to the compliance deadline if the property owner contacts the inspector before the deadline.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

If a deadline is missed

**Text:**

If a property owner misses the deadline, HHVC may charge reinspection fees. These are $256/hour for inspectors and $234/hour for technicians.

Invoices must be paid within 30 days. Unpaid bills get a $10 or $30 late penalty, and interest accrues at 1.5% per month.

Persistent violations can result in citations, civil liabilities, or a Director's Hearing to recover attorneys' fees and administrative abatement costs.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

What to expect on timing and results

**Text:**

- HHVC cannot promise an exact time or date when an inspector will call you or arrive at your building.
- We cannot promise a specific inspection finding or guarantee that a Notice of Violation will be issued.
- It does not replace a notice, citation, or legal document.
- Complaints are not assigned instantly. 311 routing and HHVC inspector assignment can take a few days and are processed on weekdays.

### Topics — \`topics\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_topicTag is unmapped metadata — the stored values are Agency labels, not Topic chooser references. Do not paste one into this chooser._

### Related — \`related\` [CHOOSE] — page not approved

_This block publishes the destination page’s title and a link, and NOTHING else. Choose the page and type no description._

1. Choose page: "Learn what Healthy Housing and Vector Control can inspect" (\`scopeInfo\` → /sf.gov/information/learn-what-hhvc-can-inspect)
2. Choose page: "Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions" (\`tenantRights\` → /sf.gov/information/tenant-rights-and-reporting-housing-conditions)

### Promote → For search engines — \`promote\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The slug is REQUIRED and lives on the Promote tab, so a page cannot be saved from the Content tab alone._

**Slug (\`slug\`):**

sf.gov/information/what-happens-after-you-report-housing-pest-problem

**Title tag (\`seo_title\`):**

