"# Free IPM education workshop — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Campaign → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`ipmEducation\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/integrated-pest-management-education\` · **Type:** Campaign

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Free IPM education workshop

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — supply the parent Agency page by hand; the mockup has no field for it._

### Spotlight 1 — \`spotlight_1\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Image alignment (Side by side / Full width) and Image position (Right / Left) are both required radios with no mockup equivalent — choose them by hand._

_Campaign shows up to 2 Spotlights. That is a rendering and editorial cap, not one the form enforces._

**Spotlight title:**

Learn how to prevent pests without heavy pesticide use

**Spotlight description:**

Healthy Housing and Vector Control offers free integrated pest management (IPM) education sessions in San Francisco. They are for tenants, resident groups, and community organizations.

IPM is a prevention-first approach. Instead of relying on heavy pesticide use, it focuses on sealing entry points and removing food and water sources. It also catches problems early.

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

This is a free City service for eligible tenant groups, resident councils, and community organizations within San Francisco.

### Spotlight 2 — \`spotlight_2\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Spotlight 1 and Spotlight 2 are two independent top-level fields, not one repeatable field with two slots, so a third spotlight concept has nowhere to go._

_Image alignment (Side by side / Full width) and Image position (Right / Left) are both required radios with no mockup equivalent — choose them by hand._

_Campaign shows up to 2 Spotlights. That is a rendering and editorial cap, not one the form enforces._

**Spotlight title:**

Request a session

**Spotlight description:**

Contact 311 to request a free IPM education session for your tenant group, resident council, or community organization. Tell them your group type, size, preferred dates, and meeting location.

You can also reach the Healthy Housing and Vector Control program directly with questions before requesting a session.

**Button link — Link text:**

Request through 311

**Button link — destination:**

External URL → https://www.sf311.org/

### Top facts — \`facts_title + fact_items\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Fact items carry no reviewer overlay — this tool records no edit for them, so these are the authored values rather than reviewed ones._

**Facts title:**

Questions before you request

**Fact 1 — title:**

Contact

**Fact 1 — text:**

Contact Healthy Housing and Vector Control by calling or texting 311, or by email at healthyhousing@sf.gov

**Fact 2 — title:**

Service area

**Fact 2 — text:**

Available to tenant groups, resident councils, and community organizations located within San Francisco

**Fact 3 — title:**

Group size

**Fact 3 — text:**

Fits groups up to about 40 residents per session; larger groups can be split into multiple sessions

**Fact 4 — title:**

Lead time

**Fact 4 — text:**

Request at least 2 weeks before your event date to allow time for scheduling and materials preparation

**Text:**

Contact Healthy Housing and Vector Control if you need help deciding whether a session is right for your group.

### Additional content — \`additional_content\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Who can request a session

**Text:**

Sessions are designed for adult residents and community members. [Healthy Housing and Vector Control](pestsTopic) can help you choose the right format for your group size and space.

- Tenant associations and resident councils
- Community centers, senior centers, and nonprofits
- Neighborhood associations and community groups
- Building resident meetings organized by tenants, not by property management

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Healthy Housing and Vector Control" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Healthy Housing and Vector Control"

### Additional content — \`additional_content\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

What the session covers

**Text:**

Sessions cover practical, low-cost steps residents can take themselves, and when to ask a landlord or the City for help.

- How to seal small gaps and cracks that let pests inside
- Safe food and garbage storage that does not attract rodents or insects
- Pick up pet waste daily. It feeds rats.
- Early signs of rats, mice, cockroaches, and bed bugs
- When to notify your landlord and when to report a problem to the City
- Least-toxic tools you can use yourself, and which pesticides to avoid indoors

### Additional content — \`additional_content\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Grounded in San Francisco health code and UC IPM guidance

**Text:**

Session content follows the same integrated pest management guidance HHVC recommends to property owners and managers. That guidance sits under Article 11 of the San Francisco Health Code.

- University of California Statewide IPM Program (UC IPM) prevention practices
- Article 11 tenant protections against unsafe or unsanitary conditions
- When a property owner is responsible for fixing a pest problem

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

See [Integrated pest management for property owners and managers](ownerGuidance) for the compliance-focused version of this guidance.

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Integrated pest management for property owners and managers" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Integrated pest management for property owners and managers"

