"# Report mold — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Transaction → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`moldReport\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/report-mold\` · **Type:** Transaction

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Page title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Page title:**

Report mold

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Visible growth on a wall or ceiling from humidity, condensation, or a leak in a home or building.

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [CHOOSE] — page not approved

_U6, narrowed — the mockup names the agency as text, not as a page reference, and Karl requires a page. Choose the parent Agency page: HHVC was approved for its own Agency page (sf.gov/departments--healthy-housing-and-vector-control), so that is the value for HHVC pages rather than Environmental Health or DPH. Those two remain Partner agencies._

1. Choose page: "Healthy Housing and Vector Control"

### Cost — \`cost\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Choose the Cost radio first (Free, Flat fee, Range, Minimum and up, None) — all five variants end at this same description field._

**Cost description:**

Free

### Things to Know — \`things_to_know\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Block 1 — Title:**

When we can act

**Block 1 — Text:**

For mold on a wall or ceiling, we can act when the affected area totals at least 10 square feet.

**Block 2 — Title:**

Mold from a leak

**Block 2 — Text:**

The Department of Building Inspection handles mold caused by a leak from a pipe, roof, or window. We handle mold caused by humidity or condensation.

### What to Do — \`what_to_do\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The mockup heading "What to do" is not typed anywhere — Karl's What to Do panel carries its own label. Each step below becomes one Section block._

_Link text is 32 characters, over the 25-character limit. Shorten it. The field itself accepts 255, so an over-length label saves without error — the form does not enforce this and will not catch it for you._

_A step with text, a button and a callout becomes ONE Section block whose Section specifics holds a Text, a Button link and a Callout as siblings — not fields on the step._

**Section 1 — Section title:**

Find the moisture source

**Section 1 — Section specifics → Text:**

Mold needs excess moisture to grow. Look for leaks from plumbing, roofs, or windows, and for condensation from humid indoor air.

If a leak is causing the mold, report it to the Department of Building Inspection instead.

**Section 1 — Section specifics → Button link → Link text:**

Report a building concern to DBI

**Section 1 — Button link → destination:**

External URL → https://www.sf.gov/report-residential-building-concern

**Section 2 — Section title:**

Tell the owner in writing

**Section 2 — Section specifics → Text:**

If you rent, tell the property owner or manager in writing as soon as you find the mold. Include the date, where the mold is, and where you think the moisture is coming from. Keep a copy of your message.

**Section 3 — Section title:**

Start your report

**Section 3 — Section specifics → Text:**

Use 311 to report mold caused by humidity or condensation. You can report mold in your home or in a shared area, or report for someone else.

Live in an SRO or residential hotel? Report the same way. One clerk takes every report and assigns SRO cases to senior inspectors.

**Section 3 — Section specifics → Button link → Link text:**

Report through 311

**Section 3 — Button link → destination:**

None

**Section 4 — Section title:**

Describe the mold

**Section 4 — Section specifics → Text:**

Include only the details that apply:

- The address, and the room or shared area
- Whether the mold is on a wall, ceiling, or somewhere else
- The size of the area, and whether it is spreading
- What you think is causing the moisture
- Whether you told the property owner or manager, if you rent
- Your contact information, if you want an inspector to contact you
- Do not rely on a third-party mold test kit when you report. Describe the visible condition and moisture source instead.

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

We never share your name or contact information. You do not need to send photos.

### Accordion title and text — \`supporting_information\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

While you wait: tips to help with the problem

**Text:**

It may take us a few weekdays to get back to you. In the meantime, here are a few things you can do to help with the problem.

- **Reduce indoor moisture:** Open windows or run fans in kitchens and bathrooms to slow mold growth.

### Custom Section — \`custom_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

⚠ Inferred mapping — verify the destination before saving.

_Inferred mapping — the field map documents this panel only for supporting/flat sections. Transaction has no generic body stream and this is its only repeatable Title-and-text panel, so verify the destination before saving._

**Title:**

What the Health Code requires

**Text:**

- San Francisco Health Code Article 11 lists visible mold or mildew inside a building as a public health nuisance. Fixing it means fixing the moisture source too.
- [View Health Code Article 11](https://codelibrary.amlegal.com/codes/san_francisco/latest/sf_health/0-0-0-1890)

