exports[`transcript Markdown, every corpus page > pestTreatmentInfo 1`] = `
"# What to expect when your building is treated for pests — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Information → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`pestTreatmentInfo\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/information/what-to-expect-when-your-building-is-treated-for-pests\` · **Type:** Information

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Page title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Page title:**

What to expect when your building is treated for pests

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Learn what tenants and owners each need to do before, during, and after a pest control visit so treatment works.

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — supply the parent Agency page by hand. Where the mockup names the agency it is text, not a page reference, and Karl requires a page: for HHVC pages that is sf.gov/departments--healthy-housing-and-vector-control._

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Who this information is for

**Text:**

- This page is for tenants and residents whose building is being treated for pests, and the owners and managers who arrange treatment.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Treatment works best when everyone helps

**Text:**

Pests move between units. If one unit is skipped or not ready, pests can come back to the whole building.

Good pest control starts with finding where pests live and closing the ways they get in. Pesticides should be the last step, not the first.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

What owners and managers must do

**Text:**

- **Look into pest reports within 72 hours**, and write each one in a log the pest control company can see.
- **Hire a licensed pest control company.** The company must also be licensed to do business in San Francisco.
- **Give tenants notice before any pesticide is used.** The notice must name the product and give the phone number for Poison Control.
- **Help tenants who cannot prepare their unit** because of a disability or medical need.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

What tenants need to do

**Text:**

- **Let the pest control company in** after you get notice of the visit.
- **Follow the checklist they give you.** Each pest and treatment can need different steps.
- **Tell them what you have seen**, like where and when you see pests.
- **Keep your home reasonably clean**, with food, garbage, and pet food stored away.
- **Ask your landlord for help** if there is a step you cannot do.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Before the visit

**Text:**

Your checklist comes first. These steps are common for most treatments.

- **Clear the floor along walls** and under sinks so the technician can reach cracks and corners.
- **Put food and dishes away** in sealed containers or the fridge.
- **Plan for children and pets.** Ask if anyone needs to leave, and for how long.
- **Ask what product will be used** if the notice does not say.
- **Ask for the treatment plan.** The pest control company should give you a plan that lists its methods and products, and what the landlord and tenants need to do.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

During and after the visit

**Text:**

Most treatments need more than one visit. Pests that were missed or hatched later may still show up for a few weeks.

- **Stay out until you are told it is safe** to come back.
- **Do not clean away baits or dusts** the technician left in cracks and corners.
- **For bed bugs, leave monitors in place.** Monitoring continues for 4 weeks after the last treatment.
- **Do not use your own sprays or bug bombs.** They can stop the treatment from working. If you buy a product yourself, choose a "Caution" label, not a "Warning" or "Danger" label.
- **Keep notes and photos** of pests you still see, and share them with your landlord in writing.

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

If you think someone was exposed to a pesticide, call Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222. Call 911 for an emergency.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

If treatment is not working

**Text:**

Tell your landlord in writing that pests are still there. If the problem continues and is not being fixed, you can report it to the City.

- [Report rats or mice](rodentsReport)
- [Report cockroaches or other insects](insectsReport)
- [Learn what happens after you report](afterReport)

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Report rats or mice" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Report rats or mice"
- "Report cockroaches or other insects" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Report cockroaches or other insects"
- "Learn what happens after you report" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "What happens after you report a housing or pest problem"

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Learn more about pest control

**Text:**

The University of California Statewide Integrated Pest Management Program (UC IPM) publishes detailed, science-based guides.

- [UC IPM: Hiring a pest control company](https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/hiring-a-pest-control-company/). What to ask and what to expect.
- [UC IPM: Understanding pesticides](https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/understanding-pesticides/). How to read a label and lower your risk.
- [UC IPM: Resources for multi-unit housing managers](https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/resources-for-multi-unit-housing-managers/). For owners and building staff.
- [California Structural Pest Control Board](https://www.pestboard.ca.gov/). Check that a pest control company is licensed.
- [SF Environment: Choosing a pest control company](https://www.sfenvironment.org/choosing-pest-control-company). How to check a license and find a company certified in integrated pest management.

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "UC IPM: Hiring a pest control company" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link
- "UC IPM: Understanding pesticides" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link
- "UC IPM: Resources for multi-unit housing managers" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link
- "California Structural Pest Control Board" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link
- "SF Environment: Choosing a pest control company" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link

### Topics — \`topics\` [TYPE] — page not approved

