exports[`transcript Markdown, every corpus page > reduceMoisture 1`] = `
"# Reduce moisture and mold in your home — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Information → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`reduceMoisture\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/information/reduce-moisture-and-mold-in-your-home\` · **Type:** Information

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Page title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Page title:**

Reduce moisture and mold in your home

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Learn what causes mold, who is responsible for fixing it, and simple daily steps that keep your home dry.

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — supply the parent Agency page by hand. Where the mockup names the agency it is text, not a page reference, and Karl requires a page: for HHVC pages that is sf.gov/departments--healthy-housing-and-vector-control._

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Who this information is for

**Text:**

- This page is for tenants, and the owners and managers of their buildings, who want to prevent or clean up mold.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The Image is a separate block in the Information section stream: add it directly after this Title and text block (+ → Image → Choose an image → Upload), then type the alt text. Information images show uncropped and need to be at least 700px wide._

**Title:**

Mold is a moisture problem

**Text:**

Tiny mold spores are in every home. They only grow into visible mold when a surface stays wet. Stop the water, and you stop the mold.

Mold can trigger asthma and allergies, especially in children and people with breathing problems. Mold can start to grow in just a couple of days, so act quickly.

**Then add an Image block — file:**

/images/mockup/mold-above-light-switch.webp (1280×960)

**Then add an Image block — alt text:**

Dark spots of mold spread across a damp wall above a light switch.

**Then add an Image block — attribution (not required):**

“Mold grows above a switch plate in this home” by FEMA, Public domain — https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flooding_%5E_Severe_Storm_-_Memphis,_Tenn._,_May_28,_2011_--_Mold_grows_above_a_switch_plate_in_this_home._FEMA_mitigation_teams_are_emphatic_about_the_dangers_of_mold_and_have_precau_-_DPLA_-_ca96e17a8de030703b06da269682a80d.jpg

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Where the water comes from

**Text:**

Who fixes mold usually depends on where the water comes from.

- **Leaks from outside**, like a leaky roof or window frame. The owner must fix these.
- **Plumbing leaks inside**, like a dripping pipe or water heater. The owner must fix these.
- **Condensation**, which is water from cooking, showers, and breathing that collects on cold walls and windows. Tenants usually manage this by keeping the air dry and moving.

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

If you see a leak, tell your landlord in writing right away. You are helping protect the building and your health.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Let damp air out

**Text:**

Fresh, moving air is your best tool against mold. These steps cost little or nothing.

- **Open windows for 5 to 15 minutes a day.** This lets damp air out and drier air in, even on a foggy day.
- **Run the fan when you cook or shower.** Leave it on for 15 to 20 minutes after. To test a fan, see if it holds up a square of toilet paper. If a fan or vent is broken, tell your landlord.
- **Put lids on pots** when you cook.
- **Dry laundry in a dryer**, not on racks indoors.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Keep air moving along walls and in closets

**Text:**

- **Move furniture at least 6 inches away from outside walls** so air can flow behind it.
- **Wipe water off windows and sills** when you see it.
- **Do not overstuff closets**, and leave closet doors a little open.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Check your humidity

**Text:**

A small gauge called a hygrometer measures how damp the air is. You can buy one for about $15 to $25.

Try to keep humidity below 50%. If it stays higher, open windows more often or use a dehumidifier.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Clean small areas of mold

**Text:**

You can clean small spots of mold yourself.

- **Use soap and water.** We do not recommend bleach.
- **Wash moldy clothes and fabrics** in warm water and dry them completely.
- **Fix the cause**, or the mold will come back.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

When to report mold to the City

**Text:**

Tell your landlord first, in writing. If your landlord does not fix a leak or a mold problem, you can report it.

- **The City can act** when mold covers at least 10 square feet of walls or ceilings. The owner must then clean it and paint with mold-resistant paint.
- **The City cannot help** with mold on personal items like furniture or clothing.
- [Report mold](moldReport)
- [Health Code Article 11 in plain language](article11Guide)

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Report mold" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Report mold"
- "Health Code Article 11 in plain language" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Health Code Article 11 in plain language"

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Learn more

**Text:**

Damp homes also attract pests that need water to survive. These guides explain how to spot and prevent them.

- [CDC: Mold](https://www.cdc.gov/mold-health/about/index.html). How mold affects health.
- [EPA: A brief guide to mold, moisture, and your home](https://www.epa.gov/mold/brief-guide-mold-moisture-and-your-home)
- [UC IPM: Cockroaches](https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/cockroaches/). Leaks and damp areas give cockroaches water.
- [UC IPM: Moth or drain flies](https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/moth-or-drain-flies/). Small flies that breed in wet, slimy drains.

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "CDC: Mold" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link
- "EPA: A brief guide to mold, moisture, and your home" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link
- "UC IPM: Cockroaches" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link
- "UC IPM: Moth or drain flies" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → External link

### Topics — \`topics\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_topicTag is unmapped metadata — the stored values are Agency labels, not Topic chooser references. Do not paste one into this chooser._

### Related — \`related\` [CHOOSE] — page not approved

