"# Keep rats and mice out of your home — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Information → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`rodentsPrevention\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/information/keep-rats-and-mice-out-of-your-home\` · **Type:** Information

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Page title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Page title:**

Keep rats and mice out of your home

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Learn the signs of rats and mice, how to cut off their food and hiding places, and what your landlord must fix.

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — supply the parent Agency page by hand. Where the mockup names the agency it is text, not a page reference, and Karl requires a page: for HHVC pages that is sf.gov/departments--healthy-housing-and-vector-control._

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Who this information is for

**Text:**

- This page is for tenants and residents who want to prevent rats and mice, or are dealing with them now.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Why rats and mice are a health risk

**Text:**

Rats and mice can spread germs through their droppings, urine, and nests. Mouse urine is also a common cause of allergies and can trigger asthma in children.

They chew through food containers and electrical wires. A small problem grows fast, so act at the first sign.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The Image is a separate block in the Information section stream: add it directly after this Title and text block (+ → Image → Choose an image → Upload), then type the alt text. Information images show uncropped and need to be at least 700px wide._

**Title:**

Signs of rats and mice

**Text:**

- **Droppings** about the size and shape of a grain of rice, often near food, under sinks, or along walls
- **Chewed food packages** or gnaw marks on wood and plastic
- **Greasy rub marks** along walls where they travel
- **Scratching sounds** in walls or ceilings, mostly at night
- **Nests** made of shredded paper or fabric

**Then add an Image block — file:**

/images/mockup/brown-rat-sidewalk.webp (1024×768)

**Then add an Image block — alt text:**

A brown rat eats crumbs on a brick sidewalk next to a building wall.

**Then add an Image block — attribution (REQUIRED by the licence):**

“Brown Rat” by oatsy40, CC BY 2.0 — https://www.flickr.com/photos/68089229@N06/7832536462

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Store food and garbage safely

**Text:**

Rats and mice stay where they can find food. Taking away food is one of the most important things you can do.

- **Store food in glass, metal, or hard plastic containers** with tight lids.
- **Keep flour, rice, and nuts in the fridge** if you store them for a long time.
- **Do not leave pet food out overnight.** Feed pets only what they will eat at one time. Store large bags of pet food in a metal garbage can with a lid.
- **Put garbage in bins with tight lids**, and take it out often.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Clean up food and water

**Text:**

- **Clean up crumbs and spills** right away, including under the stove and fridge.
- **Do not feed birds, pigeons, or other wildlife.** The food also feeds rats.
- **Fix dripping faucets**, or ask your landlord to.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Take away hiding places

**Text:**

- **Clear clutter**, like piles of paper, boxes, and bags, where mice can nest.
- **Keep storage off the floor** and away from walls when you can.
- **Keep yards and shared spaces clear** of junk, fallen fruit, and thick ivy.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Seal holes and gaps

**Text:**

A mouse can squeeze through a hole about the size of a pencil (about 1/4 inch). Rats and mice get in around pipes, vents, and gaps under doors.

In a rental, sealing the building is the owner's responsibility. Holes should be closed with metal, concrete, or steel wool covered with caulk, because rats and mice chew through foam, rubber, plastic, and wood.

- **Tell your landlord in writing** about the signs you see and any holes you find.
- **You can fill small gaps inside your home yourself.** Stuff the gap tightly with steel wool or copper mesh, then seal it with caulk.
- **Do not use spray foam.** HHVC does not recommend it, because no one can see whether steel wool was packed in first.
- **Your landlord must look into it within 72 hours** and use a licensed pest control company.
- **If nothing starts within 72 hours, report it.** If there is a serious health or safety danger, report it right away. [Report rats or mice](rodentsReport)

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

You can report anonymously. We never share your name or contact information.

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Report rats or mice" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Report rats or mice"

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Trap safely

**Text:**

- **Use snap traps indoors.** Put them along walls and in corners, out of reach of children and pets.
- **Leave traps unset for the first day.** Bait them but do not set them, so rats and mice get used to them. Then set them.
- **Check traps every day.** Keep trapping until you catch nothing for a full week.
- **Do not use glue traps.** They are inhumane.
- **Do not use poison.** It can harm children, pets, and wildlife.

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

Traps help, but they do not fix the problem. Rats and mice will keep coming back until food, hiding places, and holes are taken care of.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Clean up droppings safely

**Text:**

Wear gloves and wet droppings down with disinfectant before you wipe them up. Never dry-sweep or vacuum droppings. That puts germs into the air you breathe.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Learn more about rats and mice

**Text:**

The University of California Statewide Integrated Pest Management Program (UC IPM) publishes detailed, science-based guides.

- [UC IPM: Rats](https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/rats/). Signs, prevention, and trapping.
- [UC IPM: House mouse](https://ipm.ucanr.edu/home-and-landscape/house-mouse/). How to keep mice out of buildings.
- [CDC: How to clean up after rodents](https://www.cdc.gov/healthy-pets/rodent-control/clean-up.html). Step-by-step cleanup that keeps germs out of the air.
- [SF Environment: Managing pest problems without toxics](https://www.sfenvironment.org/managing-pest-problems-without-toxics). Simple do and do not tips for rodents and other pests.
- [UC IPM: Recursos en español](https://ipm.ucanr.edu/IPMPROJECT/recursos-espanol.html). Guías sobre plagas en español.

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

