"# Prepare your unit for pest treatment — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Step by step → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`stepPrepUnit\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/step-by-step/prepare-your-unit-for-pest-treatment\` · **Type:** Step by step

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Prepare your unit for pest treatment

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — the mockup has no primary_agency field, and Karl requires it. Supply the parent Agency page by hand: HHVC was approved for its own Agency page (sf.gov/departments--healthy-housing-and-vector-control), so that is the value for HHVC pages rather than Environmental Health or DPH. Those two remain Partner agencies._

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

What to move, wash, and bag before an exterminator treats for bed bugs or cockroaches.

### Intro — \`intro\` [TYPE] — page not approved

⚠ Inferred mapping — verify the destination before saving.

_Step by step has no things_to_know and no page-level cost, so a Transaction page's whatToKnow has nowhere to go here. Intro is the one free-text slot before the steps, which makes it where an audience line ("Who this information is for") belongs on this type._

_Inferred mapping — the field map documents this panel only for supporting/flat sections. Transaction has no generic body stream and this is its only repeatable Title-and-text panel, so verify the destination before saving._

**Title:**

Who this information is for

**Text:**

Treatment works better when the unit is ready. A visit that cannot reach the walls, floors, or furniture may have to be repeated.

- Tenants with a treatment visit scheduled
- Owners and managers giving tenants instructions
- Anyone helping prepare a unit

### Steps — \`steps\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The block 27 mockup karl notes were describing. Unlike Transaction's what_to_do Section, a Step carries its own cost and time, and a Transaction link chooser — which is how a Step by step points at the Transaction pages holding the detail._

_The mockup heading "What to do" is not typed anywhere — Karl's What to Do panel carries its own label. Each step below becomes one Section block._

_Each step is one Step block: its Title, then its Step description (text and bullets together). A step can link to one Transaction page or none, chosen with the page chooser rather than typed._

**Step 1 — Title:**

Read the instructions you were given

**Step 1 — Step description:**

Your pest control operator sends instructions for your unit and the product being used. Those instructions come first — where they differ from this page, follow them.

- Ask your owner or manager for the instructions if you did not get them.
- Ask what the product is if anyone in the home is pregnant, has asthma, or is under two.
- [Integrated pest management for property owners and managers](ownerGuidance)
- [What to expect when your building is treated for pests](pestTreatmentInfo)

**Step 2 — Title:**

Wash and bag fabric

**Step 2 — Step description:**

Bed bugs and cockroaches travel in fabric. Washing and sealing is the part that most often gets missed.

- Wash bedding, curtains, and clothing on the hottest setting the fabric allows, then dry them fully.
- Seal everything you have washed in bags and keep it sealed until treatment is finished.
- Do not move sealed bags to another unit — that is how an infestation spreads to a neighbor.

**Step 3 — Title:**

Clear access to walls, floors, and furniture

**Step 3 — Step description:**

Treatment reaches the edges of a room. Anything against a wall has to move.

- Pull furniture away from the walls.
- Empty the bottom of closets and clear the floor.
- Take everything off the floor of the room being treated.
- Vacuum floors and edges, then seal and bin the vacuum bag straight away.

**Step 4 — Title:**

Protect food, dishes, and medicine

**Step 4 — Step description:**

Anything that goes in a mouth needs to be sealed or out of the room.

- Seal food, or move it out of the rooms being treated.
- Put dishes, utensils, and food preparation surfaces away or cover them.
- Move medicine, baby bottles, and pet bowls out.

**Step 5 — Title:**

Plan for people and pets

**Step 5 — Step description:**

Ask your operator how long everyone needs to stay out and what has to happen before you go back in.

- Arrange somewhere for pets, including fish tanks and birds, which need special handling.
- Tell your operator about anyone in the home who is pregnant, older, or has a breathing condition.

**Step 6 — Title:**

After the visit

**Step 6 — Step description:**

Do not clean the treated surfaces until your operator says it is safe — cleaning too early removes the treatment.

- Keep washed items sealed until you are told otherwise.
- Report what you still see. A second visit is normal for some pests.
- [Report cockroaches or other insects](insectsReport)
- [Correct a violation and close your case](stepCorrectViolation)
- [Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions](tenantRights)

